241
00:14:55,040 --> 00:14:58,480
Honestly, I always assumed it just suspended the compiler

242
00:14:58,480 --> 00:15:02,960
or flagged the text as a string literal like you would in a Python script.

243
00:15:02,960 --> 00:15:04,960
It is actually far more elegant than that.

244
00:15:04,960 --> 00:15:10,080
The verbatim environment simply issues a command that temporarily changes the category code of almost

245
00:15:10,080 --> 00:15:12,880
every special character to category 12.

246
00:15:12,880 --> 00:15:13,680
What does 12 mean?

247
00:15:13,680 --> 00:15:18,080
Category 12 represents other. So suddenly, when the lexor encounters a backslash,

248
00:15:18,080 --> 00:15:21,520
it does not assign it category 0. It assigns it category 12.

249
00:15:21,520 --> 00:15:24,240
Oh, so it strips the backslash of its power.

250
00:15:24,880 --> 00:15:28,960
Exactly. The backslash is stripped of its power as an escape character.

251
00:15:28,960 --> 00:15:34,720
It is no longer an instruction to start a command. It is merely a printable picture of a backslash.

252
00:15:34,720 --> 00:15:36,960
And the percent sign isn't a comment anymore.

253
00:15:36,960 --> 00:15:40,640
Right. The percent sign is no longer a comment trigger. It's just a symbol.

254
00:15:40,640 --> 00:15:45,840
By manipulating the state machine of the lexor, verbatim neutralizes the code.

255
00:15:45,840 --> 00:15:50,480
That is absolutely brilliant. It literally alters the fundamental laws of physics

256
00:15:50,480 --> 00:15:54,240
for a specific block of text, then reverts them when the block ends.

257
00:15:54,240 --> 00:15:54,800
Yes.

258
00:15:54,800 --> 00:15:56,960
And here's where it gets really interesting for me.

259
00:15:57,680 --> 00:16:02,960
Once the lexor has generated this scream of poke-ins, it passes them to the expander.

260
00:16:03,760 --> 00:16:08,640
When reading this section of the source, I realized that writing latex is not like writing

261
00:16:08,640 --> 00:16:10,480
standard procedural code.

262
00:16:10,480 --> 00:16:11,200
Not at all.

263
00:16:11,200 --> 00:16:16,800
You aren't executing functions that push and pop variables to a call stack in C or JavaScript.

264
00:16:16,800 --> 00:16:21,200
You are engaging in an extreme recursive term rewriting system.

265
00:16:21,200 --> 00:16:26,000
Term rewriting is the precise computer science definition of what text does.

266
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:28,080
It is a macro expansion language.

267
00:16:28,080 --> 00:16:33,200
So if I define a custom command a macro, I'm not writing a function that returns a value.

268
00:16:33,200 --> 00:16:36,080
I am registering a rewrite rule in the engine's memory.

269
00:16:36,080 --> 00:16:36,720
Correct.

270
00:16:36,720 --> 00:16:41,120
I am telling the expander whenever you encounter this specific sequence of tokens,

271
00:16:41,120 --> 00:16:43,520
physically replace it with this other sequence of tokens.

272
00:16:43,520 --> 00:16:46,240
And this evaluation is inherently recursive.

273
00:16:46,240 --> 00:16:49,840
Once the expander replaces your macro with its defined token sequence,

274
00:16:49,840 --> 00:16:52,320
it immediately examines the new sequence.

275
00:16:52,320 --> 00:16:54,400
Right. To see if there are more macros inside it.

276
00:16:54,400 --> 00:16:55,040
Exactly.

277
00:16:55,040 --> 00:16:58,960
If that new sequence contains further macros, it replaces those as well.

278
00:16:58,960 --> 00:17:04,400
It acts like an abstract syntax tree resolution, continuously expanding the nodes until only

279
00:17:04,400 --> 00:17:10,240
unexpandable primitives remain the literal boxes, glue, fonts, and penalties we discussed earlier.

280
00:17:10,240 --> 00:17:14,880
It just keeps digging down, finding, and replacing until it hits bedrock.

281
00:17:14,880 --> 00:17:16,400
And this expansion is lazy, right?

282
00:17:16,400 --> 00:17:17,200
Extremely lazy.

283
00:17:17,200 --> 00:17:20,800
The engine only expands a token when it is absolutely forced to.

284
00:17:20,800 --> 00:17:21,920
Why is that important?

285
00:17:21,920 --> 00:17:27,680
Because you can pass a complex macro as an argument into another macro, and the engine will not

286
00:17:27,680 --> 00:17:32,320
evaluate the inner macro until the exact moment the outer macro decides to utilize it.

287
00:17:32,320 --> 00:17:33,200
Oh, I see.

288
00:17:33,200 --> 00:17:39,680
This lazy evaluation allows for incredibly complex logic, but it also creates unique debugging

289
00:17:39,680 --> 00:17:43,040
nightmares, which we will explore when we discuss gradual commands.

290
00:17:43,040 --> 00:17:48,000
Okay. So we have established the theoretical bedrock. We have a physics engine of boxes and glue,

